  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "$\\,$ $|x-2|<6$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n\nI. $|x|<4$\n\nII. $|x|<8$\n\nIII. $x=2$",
  "worked_solution": "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,(|x-2|<6)\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-2| & <6 \\\\ -6<x-2 & <6 \\\\ -4<x & <8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions. \n\n- Condition I is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(|x|<4)\\,⇒\\,(|x-2|<6)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $|x|<4,$ we have $-4<x<4,$ therefore $-4<x<8.$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(|x|<8)\\,⇏\\,(|x-2|<6)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-7,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-2| & =|-7-2| \\\\  & =9≮6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition III is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(x=2)\\,⇒\\,(|x-2|<6)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $x=2,$ then $|x-2|=0<6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
